## ---
   private: true
   ---
   A date-time with a timezone in the ISO-8601 calendar system, such as
   "2007-12-03T10:15:30+01:00 Europe/Paris".
   Time is a representation of a date-time with a timezone. This class stores all
   date and time fields, to a precision of nanoseconds, and a timezone, with a
   zone offset used to handle ambiguous local date-times.
   For example, the value "2nd October 2007 at 13:45.30.123456789 +02:00 in the
   Europe/Paris timezone" can be stored as `Time`.
@Builtin_Type
type Date_Time
    ## ---
       icon: time
       ---
       The start of the Enso epoch, 15 October 1582, at midnight UTC.

       ## Examples
       ### The start of the Enso epoch
       ```
       start = Date_Time.enso_epoch_start
       # → 1582-10-15 00:00:00Z[UTC]
       ```

       ## Remarks

       ### Start of the epoch
       For Enso, start of the epoch is equal to the start of the Gregorian
       calendar, which is on 15th October 1582.
       Invoking some Gregorian calendar related functionalities, like
       `is_leap_year`, is computed for all the date times, including those before
       an epoch start, with today's rules. Trying to get some Gregorian calendar
       related properties for a historical date times that is defined before the
       epoch is likely an error, as the Gregorian calendar had not yet been
       introduced. Therefore, for such historical date times, a warning is
       attached to the result.
    enso_epoch_start -> Date_Time = Date_Helpers.date_time_enso_epoch_start

    ## ---
       aliases: [current time]
       group: DateTime
       icon: time
       suggested: 11
       ---
       The current date and time, from the system clock in the system timezone.

       ## Examples
       ### The current date and time
       ```
       time = Date_Time.now
       ```
    now -> Date_Time = Date_Helpers.date_time_now

    ## ---
       group: Constants
       icon: time
       arguments:
         month: always
         day: always
         hour: always
         minute: always
       ---
       Constructs a new `Date_Time` from a year, month, day, hour, minute, second,
       millisecond, microsecond, nanosecond and timezone.

       Returns a `Time_Error` if the provided time cannot be represented.

       ## Arguments
       - `year`: The year to represent. Any integer is valid.
       - `month`: The month-of-year to represent, from 1 (January) to 12 (December).
       - `day`: The day-of-month to represent, from 1 to 31. It must be valid for the
         year and month.
       - `hour`: The hour-of-day to represent, from 0 to 23.
       - `minute`: The minute-of-hour to represent, from 0 to 59.
       - `second`: The second-of-minute to represent, from 0 to 59.
       - `millisecond`: The millisecond-of-second to represent, from 0 to 999.
       - `microsecond`: The microsecond-of-millisecond to represent, from 0 to 999.
       - `nanosecond`: The nanosecond-of-microsecond to represent, from 0 to 999.
       - `zone`: The timezone. By default it is the system timezone.

       ## Examples
       ### A date-time from its parts
       ```
       time = Date_Time.new 2020 12 23 11 45 zone=Time_Zone.utc
       # → 2020-12-23 11:45:00Z[UTC]
       ```

       ### The start of a year
       ```
       time = Date_Time.new 1970 zone=Time_Zone.utc
       # → 1970-01-01 00:00:00Z[UTC]
       ```

       ### A time with a fraction of a second
       ```
       time = Date_Time.new 2020 12 23 11 45 30 millisecond=250 zone=Time_Zone.utc
       # → 2020-12-23 11:45:30.25Z[UTC]
       ```

       ### A date that does not exist
       ```
       time = Date_Time.new 2020 2 30
       result = time.catch Any _-> "not a date"
       # → "not a date"
       ```
    @year (Widget.Numeric_Input)
    @month (Widget.Numeric_Input minimum=1 maximum=12)
    @day make_day_picker
    @hour (Widget.Numeric_Input minimum=0 maximum=23)
    @minute (Widget.Numeric_Input minimum=0 maximum=59)
    @second (Widget.Numeric_Input minimum=0 maximum=59)
    @millisecond (Widget.Numeric_Input minimum=0 maximum=999)
    @microsecond (Widget.Numeric_Input minimum=0 maximum=999)
    @nanosecond (Widget.Numeric_Input minimum=0 maximum=999)
    new (year:Integer=(Missing_Argument.throw "year")) (month:Integer=1) (day:Integer=1) (hour:Integer=0) (minute:Integer=0) (second:Integer=0) (millisecond:Integer=0) (microsecond:Integer=0) (nanosecond:Integer=0) (zone:Time_Zone=Time_Zone.system) -> Date_Time ! Time_Error =
        total_nanoseconds = nanosecond + microsecond * 1000 + millisecond * 1000000
        Panic.catch JException (Date_Helpers.date_time_new_builtin year month day hour minute second total_nanoseconds zone) cause->
            Error.throw (Time_Error.Error cause.payload.getMessage)

    ## ---
       aliases: [time from text]
       group: Conversions
       icon: convert
       arguments:
         format: always
       ---
       Converts text containing a date and time into a `Date_Time`.

       Returns a `Time_Error` if the provided `text` cannot be parsed using the
       provided `format`.

       ## Arguments
       - `text`: The text to try and parse as a date-time.
       - `format`: A pattern describing how to parse the text, or a
         `Date_Time_Formatter`. By default the text is read as an ISO-8601 date-time
         with an optional zone, such as `2007-12-03T10:15:30+01:00[Europe/Paris]`.

       ## Examples
       ### Parse a UTC date-time
       ```
       time = Date_Time.parse "2020-10-01T04:11:12Z"
       # → 2020-10-01 04:11:12Z
       ```

       ### Parse a date-time with an offset
       ```
       time = Date_Time.parse "2020-10-01T04:11:12-04:00"
       # → 2020-10-01 04:11:12-04:00
       ```

       ### Parse a date-time with an offset and a timezone
       ```
       time = Date_Time.parse "2020-10-01T04:11:12-04:00[America/New_York]"
       # → 2020-10-01 04:11:12-04:00[America/New_York]
       ```

       ### Parse a date-time with a fraction of a second
       ```
       time = Date_Time.parse "2020-10-01T04:11:12.177528-04:00"
       # → 2020-10-01 04:11:12.177528-04:00
       ```

       ### Parse a date-time with a custom format
       ```
       time = Date_Time.parse "2020-05-06 04:30:20" "yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm:ss"
       ```

       ### Parse a date-time with words in it
       ```
       time = Date_Time.parse "06 of May 2020 at 04:30AM" "dd 'of' MMMM yyyy 'at' hh:mma"
       ```

       ### Fall back to the current time when the text is not a date-time
       ```
       parsed = Date_Time.parse "2020-10-01"
       time = parsed.catch Any _-> Date_Time.now
       ```

       ## Remarks

       ### Pattern Syntax
       If the pattern is provided as `Text`, it is parsed using the format
       described below. See `Date_Time_Formatter` for more options.
       - y: Year. The number of pattern letters determines the minimum number of
       digits.
       - y: The year using any number of digits.
       - yy: The year, using at most two digits. The default range is 1950-2049,
       but this can be changed by including the end year in braces e.g.
       `yy{2099}`.
       - yyyy: The year, using exactly four digits.
       - M: Month of year. The number of pattern letters determines the format:
       - M: Any number (1-12).
       - MM: Month number with zero padding required (01-12).
       - MMM: Short name of the month (Jan-Dec).
       - MMMM: Full name of the month (January-December).
       The month names depend on the selected locale.
       - d: Day. The number of pattern letters determines the format:
       - d: Any number (1-31).
       - dd: Day number with zero padding required (01-31).
       - ddd: Short name of the day of week (Mon-Sun).
       - dddd: Full name of the day of week (Monday-Sunday).
       The weekday names depend on the selected locale.
       Both day of week and day of month may be included in a single pattern - in
       such case the day of week is used as a sanity check.
       - Q: Quarter of year.
       If only year and quarter are provided in the pattern, when parsing a date,
       the result will be the first day of that quarter.
       - H: 24h hour of day (0-23).
       - h: 12h hour of day (0-12). The `a` pattern is needed to disambiguate
       between AM and PM.
       - m: Minute of hour.
       - s: Second of minute.
       - f: Fractional part of the second. The number of pattern letters
       determines the number of digits. If one letter is used, any number of
       digits will be accepted.
       - a: AM/PM marker.
       - T: If repeated 3 or less times - Time zone ID (e.g. Europe/Warsaw, Z,
       -08:30), otherwise - Time zone name (e.g. Central European Time, CET).
       - Z: Zone offset.
       - Z, ZZ, ZZZ: A short offset form (+HHmm).
       No offset is indicated by "+0000". This can be customized by setting an
       alternative no offset string in curly braces, e.g. `zz{Z}`.
       - ZZZZ: Localized offset (e.g. GMT-08:00).
       - ZZZZZ: A full offset form (+HH:mm:ss).
       No offset is indicated by "Z". This can be customized as above, e.g.
       `ZZZZZ{0}`.

       ### Default Date_Time Format
       Unless you provide a custom format, the text must represent a valid
       date-time as defined by the ISO-8601 format (see
       https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ISO_8601).
       If a time zone is present, it must be in the ISO-8601 Extended Date/Time
       Format (EDTF) (see https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ISO_8601#EDTF).
       The time zone format consists of:
       - The ISO offset date time.
       - If the zone ID is not available or is a zone offset then the format is
       complete.
       - An open square bracket '['.
       - The zone ID. This is not part of the ISO-8601 standard. Parsing is case
       sensitive.
       - A close square bracket ']'.
    @format make_date_time_format_selector
    parse (text : Text) (format : Date_Time_Formatter = Date_Time_Formatter.default_enso_zoned_date_time) -> Date_Time ! Time_Error =
        format.parse_date_time text

    ## ---
       icon: convert
       ---
       Creates a `Date_Time` from a Unix epoch timestamp in seconds, and optionally
       nanoseconds. The result is in UTC.

       ## Arguments
       - `seconds`: The number of seconds since the Unix epoch, 1 January 1970 UTC.
       - `nanoseconds`: The number of nanoseconds to add within the second.

       ## Examples
       ### A date-time from a Unix timestamp
       ```
       time = Date_Time.from_unix_epoch_seconds 1601587200
       # → 2020-10-01 21:20:00Z[UTC]
       ```
    from_unix_epoch_seconds (seconds : Integer) (nanoseconds : Integer = 0) -> Date_Time =
        unix_epoch_start + Duration.new seconds=seconds nanoseconds=nanoseconds

    ## ---
       icon: convert
       ---
       Creates a `Date_Time` from a Unix epoch timestamp in milliseconds. The result is
       in UTC.

       ## Arguments
       - `milliseconds`: The number of milliseconds since the Unix epoch, 1 January
         1970 UTC.

       ## Examples
       ### A date-time from a Unix timestamp in milliseconds
       ```
       time = Date_Time.from_unix_epoch_milliseconds 1601587200000
       # → 2020-10-01 21:20:00Z[UTC]
       ```
    from_unix_epoch_milliseconds (milliseconds : Integer) -> Date_Time =
        unix_epoch_start + Duration.new milliseconds=milliseconds

    ## ---
       group: Metadata
       icon: metadata
       ---
       The year of this date-time.

       ## Examples
       ### The year of a date-time
       ```
       time = Date_Time.new 2020 12 23 11 45 30
       year = time.year
       # → 2020
       ```
    year self -> Integer = Date_Helpers.date_time_year self

    ## ---
       group: Metadata
       icon: metadata
       ---
       The month of this date-time, as a number from 1 (January) to 12 (December).

       ## Examples
       ### The month of a date-time
       ```
       time = Date_Time.new 2020 12 23 11 45 30
       month = time.month
       # → 12
       ```
    month self -> Integer = Date_Helpers.date_time_month self

    ## ---
       group: Metadata
       icon: metadata
       ---
       The day of the month of this date-time, from 1 to 31.

       ## Examples
       ### The day of the month of a date-time
       ```
       time = Date_Time.new 2020 12 23 11 45 30
       day = time.day
       # → 23
       ```
    day self -> Integer = Date_Helpers.date_time_day self

    ## ---
       group: Metadata
       icon: metadata
       ---
       The hour of this date-time, from 0 to 23.

       ## Examples
       ### The hour of a date-time
       ```
       time = Date_Time.new 2020 12 23 11 45 30
       hour = time.hour
       # → 11
       ```
    hour self -> Integer = Date_Helpers.date_time_hour self

    ## ---
       group: Metadata
       icon: metadata
       ---
       The minute of this date-time, from 0 to 59.

       ## Examples
       ### The minute of a date-time
       ```
       time = Date_Time.new 2020 12 23 11 45 30
       minute = time.minute
       # → 45
       ```
    minute self -> Integer = Date_Helpers.date_time_minute self

    ## ---
       group: Metadata
       icon: metadata
       ---
       The second of this date-time, from 0 to 59.

       ## Examples
       ### The second of a date-time
       ```
       time = Date_Time.new 2020 12 23 11 45 30
       second = time.second
       # → 30
       ```
    second self -> Integer = Date_Helpers.date_time_second self

    ## ---
       group: Metadata
       icon: metadata
       ---
       The millisecond part of the second of this date-time, from 0 to 999.

       ## Examples
       ### The millisecond of a date-time
       ```
       time = Date_Time.new 2020 12 23 11 45 30 millisecond=250
       millisecond = time.millisecond
       # → 250
       ```
    millisecond self -> Integer = Date_Helpers.date_time_millisecond self

    ## ---
       group: Metadata
       icon: metadata
       ---
       The microsecond part of the millisecond of this date-time, from 0 to 999.

       ## Examples
       ### The microsecond of a date-time
       ```
       time = Date_Time.new 2020 12 23 11 45 30 millisecond=250 microsecond=125
       microsecond = time.microsecond
       # → 125
       ```
    microsecond self -> Integer = Date_Helpers.date_time_microsecond self

    ## ---
       group: Metadata
       icon: metadata
       ---
       The nanosecond part of the microsecond of this date-time, from 0 to 999.

       ## Arguments
       - `include_milliseconds`: Whether to return the whole fractional part of the
         second, in nanoseconds, instead of just the part below a microsecond.

       ## Examples
       ### The nanosecond of a date-time
       ```
       time = Date_Time.new 2020 12 23 11 45 30 millisecond=250 microsecond=125 nanosecond=7
       nanosecond = time.nanosecond
       # → 7
       ```

       ### The whole fraction of a second, in nanoseconds
       ```
       time = Date_Time.new 2020 12 23 11 45 30 millisecond=250 microsecond=125 nanosecond=7
       nanoseconds = time.nanosecond include_milliseconds=True
       # → 250125007
       ```
    nanosecond self include_milliseconds:Boolean=False -> Integer =
        Date_Helpers.date_time_nanosecond self include_milliseconds

    ## ---
       group: DateTime
       icon: metadata
       ---
       The timezone of this date-time.

       ## Examples
       ### The timezone of a date-time
       ```
       time = Date_Time.new 2020 12 23 11 45 zone=Time_Zone.utc
       zone = time.zone
       # → UTC
       ```
    zone self -> Time_Zone = Date_Helpers.date_time_zone self

    ## ---
       icon: convert
       ---
       The number of seconds from the start of the Unix epoch, 1 January 1970 UTC, to
       this date-time. It is negative for a date-time before the epoch.

       ## Examples
       ### The Unix timestamp of a date-time
       ```
       time = Date_Time.new 2020 10 1 zone=Time_Zone.utc
       seconds = time.to_unix_epoch_seconds
       # → 1601510400
       ```
    to_unix_epoch_seconds self -> Integer =
        (Duration.between unix_epoch_start self).total_seconds.floor

    ## ---
       icon: convert
       ---
       The number of milliseconds from the start of the Unix epoch, 1 January 1970 UTC,
       to this date-time. It is negative for a date-time before the epoch.

       ## Examples
       ### The Unix timestamp of a date-time in milliseconds
       ```
       time = Date_Time.new 2020 10 1 zone=Time_Zone.utc
       milliseconds = time.to_unix_epoch_milliseconds
       # → 1601510400000
       ```
    to_unix_epoch_milliseconds self -> Integer =
        (Duration.between unix_epoch_start self).total_milliseconds.floor

    ## ---
       icon: convert
       ---
       The number of seconds from the start of the Enso epoch to this date-time. See
       `Date_Time.enso_epoch_start`.

       ## Examples
       ### A day after the start of the Enso epoch
       ```
       time = Date_Time.new 1582 10 16 zone=Time_Zone.utc
       seconds = time.to_enso_epoch_seconds
       # → 86400
       ```
    to_enso_epoch_seconds self -> Integer =
        (Duration.between Date_Time.enso_epoch_start self).total_seconds.floor

    ## ---
       icon: convert
       ---
       The number of milliseconds from the start of the Enso epoch to this date-time.
       See `Date_Time.enso_epoch_start`.

       ## Examples
       ### A day after the start of the Enso epoch
       ```
       time = Date_Time.new 1582 10 16 zone=Time_Zone.utc
       milliseconds = time.to_enso_epoch_milliseconds
       # → 86400000
       ```
    to_enso_epoch_milliseconds self -> Integer =
        (Duration.between Date_Time.enso_epoch_start self).total_milliseconds.floor

    ## ---
       group: DateTime
       icon: metadata
       ---
       The time of day of this date-time, discarding the date and the timezone.

       ## Examples
       ### The time of day of a date-time
       ```
       time = Date_Time.new 2020 12 23 11 45 30
       time_of_day = time.time_of_day
       # → 11:45:30
       ```
    time_of_day self -> Time_Of_Day =
        Date_Helpers.date_time_of_day self

    ## ---
       icon: metadata
       ---
       The number of the week of the year this date-time falls into.
       Produces a warning for a date-time that is before epoch start.

       ## Arguments
       - `locale`: The locale that defines the notion of weeks of year. If no locale
         is given, the ISO 8601 week of year is used.

       ## Examples
       ### The ISO week of a date-time at the start of a year
       ```
       time = Date_Time.new 2021 1 1 11 45
       week = time.week_of_year
       # → 53
       ```

       ### The week of the same date-time in the US
       ```
       time = Date_Time.new 2021 1 1 11 45
       week = time.week_of_year Locale.us
       # → 1
       ```

       ## Remarks

       ### Locale Dependency
       Note that this operation is locale-specific. It varies both by the local
       definition of the first day of week and the definition of the first week
       of year. For example, in the US, the first day of the week is Sunday and
       week 1 is the week containing January 1. In the UK on the other hand, the
       first day of the week is Monday, and week 1 is the week containing the
       first Thursday of the year. Therefore it is important to properly specify
       the `locale` argument.
    week_of_year self (locale : Locale | Nothing = Nothing) -> Integer =
        ensure_in_epoch self <|
            if locale.is_nothing then Time_Utils.get_field_as_zoneddatetime self IsoFields.WEEK_OF_WEEK_BASED_YEAR else
                Time_Utils.week_of_year_zoneddatetime self locale.java_locale

    ## ---
       group: DateTime
       icon: metadata
       ---
       Whether this date-time is in a leap year.
       Produces a warning for a date-time that is before epoch start.

       ## Examples
       ### A leap year
       ```
       time = Date_Time.new 2000 3 1
       is_leap = time.is_leap_year
       # → True
       ```

       ### A century that is not a leap year
       ```
       time = Date_Time.new 1900 3 1
       is_leap = time.is_leap_year
       # → False
       ```
    is_leap_year self -> Boolean = self.date.is_leap_year

    ## ---
       group: DateTime
       icon: metadata
       ---
       The number of days in the year of this date-time, 365 or 366.
       Produces a warning for a date-time that is before epoch start.

       ## Examples
       ### The length of a leap year
       ```
       time = Date_Time.new 2020 3 1
       days = time.length_of_year
       # → 366
       ```

       ### The length of an ordinary year
       ```
       time = Date_Time.new 2021 3 1
       days = time.length_of_year
       # → 365
       ```
    length_of_year self -> Integer = ensure_in_epoch self <| self.date.length_of_year

    ## ---
       icon: metadata
       ---
       The century of this date-time, counting 2000 as the last year of the 20th
       century.
       Returns an `Illegal_Argument` error for a year before the common era.

       ## Examples
       ### The century of the year 2000
       ```
       time = Date_Time.new 2000 1 1
       century = time.century
       # → 20
       ```
    century self -> Integer = self.date.century

    ## ---
       group: Metadata
       icon: metadata
       ---
       The quarter of the year this date-time falls into, from 1 to 4.

       ## Examples
       ### The quarter of a date-time
       ```
       time = Date_Time.new 2020 12 23 11 45 30
       quarter = time.quarter
       # → 4
       ```
    quarter self -> Integer = Time_Utils.get_field_as_zoneddatetime self IsoFields.QUARTER_OF_YEAR

    ## ---
       group: DateTime
       icon: metadata
       ---
       The number of days in the month of this date-time.
       Produces a warning for a date-time that is before epoch start.

       ## Examples
       ### The length of a leap-year February
       ```
       time = Date_Time.new 2020 2 10
       days = time.length_of_month
       # → 29
       ```
    length_of_month self -> Integer = ensure_in_epoch self <| self.date.length_of_month

    ## ---
       group: DateTime
       icon: metadata
       ---
       The first day of the month of this date-time, as a `Date`.

       ## Examples
       ### The first day of December
       ```
       time = Date_Time.new 2020 12 23 11 45 30
       first = time.first_of_month
       # → 2020-12-01
       ```
    first_of_month self -> Date =
        Time_Utils.first_of_month self

    ## ---
       group: DateTime
       icon: metadata
       ---
       The last day of the month of this date-time, as a `Date`.

       ## Examples
       ### The last day of a leap-year February
       ```
       time = Date_Time.new 2020 2 10 11 45
       last = time.end_of_month
       # → 2020-02-29
       ```
    end_of_month self -> Date =
        Time_Utils.end_of_month self

    ## ---
       icon: metadata
       ---
       The day of the year of this date-time, counting 1 January as day 1.

       ## Examples
       ### The last day of a leap year
       ```
       time = Date_Time.new 2020 12 31 11 45
       day = time.day_of_year
       # → 366
       ```
    day_of_year self -> Integer = Time_Utils.get_field_as_zoneddatetime self ChronoField.DAY_OF_YEAR

    ## ---
       group: Metadata
       icon: metadata
       ---
       The day of the week of this date-time.
       Produces a warning for a date-time that is before epoch start.

       ## Examples
       ### The day of the week of a date-time
       ```
       time = Date_Time.new 2020 12 23 11 45
       day = time.day_of_week
       # → Wednesday
       ```
    day_of_week self -> Day_Of_Week =
        ensure_in_epoch self <|
            Day_Of_Week.from (Time_Utils.get_field_as_zoneddatetime self ChronoField.DAY_OF_WEEK) Day_Of_Week.Monday

    ## ---
       group: DateTime
       icon: time
       ---
       The first moment of the period of the given kind that contains this date-time.

       ## Arguments
       - `period`: The kind of period to find the start of. A `Date_Period` (year,
         quarter, month, week or day) gives midnight on its first day, a `Time_Period`
         (hour down to nanosecond) gives the start of that hour, minute and so on.

       ## Examples
       ### The start of the quarter
       ```
       time = Date_Time.new 2020 12 23 11 45 30 zone=Time_Zone.utc
       start = time.start_of Date_Period.Quarter
       # → 2020-10-01 00:00:00Z[UTC]
       ```

       ### The start of the hour
       ```
       time = Date_Time.new 2020 12 23 11 45 30 zone=Time_Zone.utc
       start = time.start_of Time_Period.Hour
       # → 2020-12-23 11:00:00Z[UTC]
       ```
    @period make_date_time_period_selector
    start_of self (period : Date_Period | Time_Period = Date_Period.Month) -> Date_Time =
        adjusted = period.adjust_start self
        case period of
            _ : Date_Period -> Date_Period.Day.adjust_start adjusted
            _ : Time_Period -> adjusted

    ## ---
       group: DateTime
       icon: time
       ---
       The last moment of the period of the given kind that contains this date-time,
       to the nanosecond.

       ## Arguments
       - `period`: The kind of period to find the end of. A `Date_Period` (year,
         quarter, month, week or day) gives the end of its last day, a `Time_Period`
         (hour down to nanosecond) gives the end of that hour, minute and so on.

       ## Examples
       ### The end of the month
       ```
       time = Date_Time.new 2020 12 23 11 45 30 zone=Time_Zone.utc
       last = time.end_of Date_Period.Month
       # → 2020-12-31 23:59:59.999999999Z[UTC]
       ```

       ### The end of the hour
       ```
       time = Date_Time.new 2020 12 23 11 45 30 zone=Time_Zone.utc
       last = time.end_of Time_Period.Hour
       # → 2020-12-23 11:59:59.999999999Z[UTC]
       ```
    @period make_date_time_period_selector
    end_of self (period : Date_Period | Time_Period = Date_Period.Month) -> Date_Time =
        adjusted = period.adjust_end self
        case period of
            _ : Date_Period -> Date_Period.Day.adjust_end adjusted
            _ : Time_Period -> adjusted

    ## ---
       aliases: [time to date]
       group: DateTime
       icon: metadata
       ---
       The date of this date-time, discarding the time of day and the timezone.

       ## Examples
       ### The date of a date-time
       ```
       time = Date_Time.new 2020 12 23 11 45 30
       date = time.date
       # → 2020-12-23
       ```
    date self -> Date = Date_Helpers.date_time_date self

    ## ---
       aliases: [change time zone]
       group: DateTime
       icon: time
       ---
       The same instant as this date-time, written in the provided timezone.

       ## Arguments
       - `zone`: The timezone to convert the date-time into.

       ## Examples
       ### Midday UTC as a time at UTC-04:00
       ```
       time = Date_Time.new 2020 1 1 12 zone=Time_Zone.utc
       local = time.at_time_zone (Time_Zone.new -4)
       # → 2020-01-01 08:00:00-04:00
       ```
    at_time_zone self (zone : Time_Zone) -> Date_Time =
        Time_Utils.with_zone_same_instant self zone

    ## ---
       aliases: [change time zone]
       group: DateTime
       icon: time
       ---
       Changes the timezone of this date-time without changing its local date and
       time, which makes it a different instant.

       ## Arguments
       - `zone`: The timezone to put the date-time into.

       ## Examples
       ### Keep the local time and change the zone
       ```
       time = Date_Time.new 2020 1 1 12 zone=Time_Zone.utc
       local = time.set_time_zone (Time_Zone.new -4)
       # → 2020-01-01 12:00:00-04:00
       ```
    set_time_zone self (zone : Time_Zone) -> Date_Time =
        Time_Utils.with_zone_same_local self zone

    ## ---
       aliases: [change time zone]
       group: DateTime
       icon: time
       ---
       The same instant as this date-time, written in UTC.

       ## Examples
       ### A Moscow time in UTC
       ```
       time = Date_Time.new 2020 6 1 12 zone=(Time_Zone.parse "Europe/Moscow")
       utc = time.to_utc
       # → 2020-06-01 09:00:00Z[UTC]
       ```
    to_utc self -> Date_Time =
        self.at_time_zone Time_Zone.utc

    ## ---
       group: DateTime
       icon: time
       ---
       A part of this date-time, as a number: the year, quarter, month, week of the
       year or day of the month, or the hour, minute, second, millisecond, microsecond
       or nanosecond.
       Produces a warning for a date-time that is before epoch start.
       See `Date_Time.enso_epoch_start`.

       ## Arguments
       - `period`: Which part of the date-time to return.

       ## Examples
       ### The quarter of a date-time
       ```
       time = Date_Time.new 2020 12 23 11 45
       quarter = time.date_part Date_Period.Quarter
       # → 4
       ```

       ### The minute of a date-time
       ```
       time = Date_Time.new 2020 12 23 11 45
       minute = time.date_part Time_Period.Minute
       # → 45
       ```
    @period make_date_time_period_selector
    date_part self (period : Date_Period | Time_Period) -> Integer =
        case period of
            Date_Period.Year        -> self.year
            Date_Period.Quarter     -> self.quarter
            Date_Period.Month       -> self.month
            Date_Period.Week _      -> self.week_of_year locale=Nothing
            Date_Period.Day         -> self.day
            Time_Period.Hour        -> self.hour
            Time_Period.Minute      -> self.minute
            Time_Period.Second      -> self.second
            Time_Period.Millisecond -> self.millisecond
            Time_Period.Microsecond -> self.microsecond
            Time_Period.Nanosecond  -> self.nanosecond

    ## ---
       group: DateTime
       icon: time
       ---
       Computes the difference between two date-times as a whole number of periods.
       The difference is positive if `end` is after this date-time.
       Produces a warning for a date-time that is before epoch start.
       See `Date_Time.enso_epoch_start`.

       ## Arguments
       - `end`: The date-time to compute the difference to.
       - `period`: The kind of period to count.

       ## Examples
       ### The whole months between two date-times
       ```
       time = Date_Time.new 2020 1 1 zone=Time_Zone.utc
       finish = Date_Time.new 2020 12 23 zone=Time_Zone.utc
       months = time.date_diff finish Date_Period.Month
       # → 11
       ```

       ### The whole hours between two date-times
       ```
       time = Date_Time.new 2020 12 23 8 30 zone=Time_Zone.utc
       finish = Date_Time.new 2020 12 23 17 15 zone=Time_Zone.utc
       hours = time.date_diff finish Time_Period.Hour
       # → 8
       ```
    @period make_date_time_period_selector
    date_diff self (end : Date_Time) (period : Date_Period | Time_Period) -> Integer = ensure_in_epoch self <|
        Time_Utils.unit_datetime_difference period.to_java_unit self end

    ## ---
       group: DateTime
       icon: time
       ---
       Shifts this date-time by a number of periods.
       Produces a warning for a date-time that is before epoch start.
       See `Date_Time.enso_epoch_start`.

       ## Arguments
       - `amount`: How many periods to shift the date-time by. A negative number shifts
         it back.
       - `period`: The kind of period to shift by.

       ## Examples
       ### A month after the end of January
       ```
       time = Date_Time.new 2020 1 31 11 45 zone=Time_Zone.utc
       shifted = time.date_add 1 Date_Period.Month
       # → 2020-02-29 11:45:00Z[UTC]
       ```

       ### Three hours earlier
       ```
       time = Date_Time.new 2020 1 1 1 30 zone=Time_Zone.utc
       shifted = time.date_add -3 Time_Period.Hour
       # → 2019-12-31 22:30:00Z[UTC]
       ```
    @period make_date_time_period_selector
    date_add self (amount : Integer) (period : Date_Period | Time_Period) -> Date_Time = ensure_in_epoch self <|
        Time_Utils.unit_datetime_add period.to_java_unit self amount

    ## ---
       aliases: [add duration, add period]
       group: Operators
       icon: time
       ---
       Adds an amount of time to this date-time to get another date-time.

       ## Arguments
       - `amount`: The amount of time to add: a `Duration`, a `Period` for a custom
         number of years, months and days, or a `Time_Period` or `Date_Period` for one
         unit.

       ## Examples
       ### Add 15 years and 3 hours
       ```
       time = Date_Time.new 2020 zone=Time_Zone.utc
       later = time + (Period.new years=15) + (Duration.new hours=3)
       # → 2035-01-01 03:00:00Z[UTC]
       ```

       ### Add a quarter
       ```
       time = Date_Time.new 2020 zone=Time_Zone.utc
       later = time + Date_Period.Quarter
       # → 2020-04-01 00:00:00Z[UTC]
       ```
    @amount (make_date_time_period_selector include_custom=True)
    + self (amount : Duration | Period | Time_Period | Date_Period) -> Date_Time ! Time_Error =
        case amount of
            duration : Duration ->
                Panic.catch ArithmeticException (Date_Helpers.date_time_plus self duration) (err -> Error.throw (Time_Error.Error err.getMessage))
            period : Period ->
                Time_Utils.datetime_adjust self Time_Utils.AdjustOp.PLUS period.internal_period
            time_period : Time_Period ->
                self + time_period.to_duration
            date_period : Date_Period ->
                self + date_period.to_period

    ## ---
       group: DateTime
       icon: time
       ---
       Shifts this date-time by a number of working days, Monday to Friday. The time of
       day is kept.
       Produces a warning for a date-time that is before epoch start.
       See `Date_Time.enso_epoch_start`.

       ## Arguments
       - `days`: The number of working days to shift by. A negative number shifts back.
         If it is zero, the date-time is kept, unless it falls on a weekend or a
         holiday, in which case the next working day is returned.
       - `holidays`: Dates to skip as well. Weekends are already skipped.

       ## Examples
       ### Five working days after a Monday
       ```
       time = Date_Time.new 2020 2 3 11 45 zone=Time_Zone.utc
       later = time.add_work_days 5
       # → 2020-02-10 11:45:00Z[UTC]
       ```

       ### Skipping a holiday
       ```
       time = Date_Time.new 2020 2 3 11 45 zone=Time_Zone.utc
       holidays = [Date.new 2020 2 5]
       later = time.add_work_days 5 holidays
       # → 2020-02-11 11:45:00Z[UTC]
       ```

       ## Remarks

       ### Weekends
       The result is always a working day. A shift of zero from a Saturday or Sunday
       gives the Monday after. A shift of one from a Sunday gives the Tuesday, so that
       the Monday is a whole working day within the interval. A shift of one day back
       from a Saturday, a Sunday or a Monday gives the Friday before.

       This is consistent with `Date.work_days_until`: `date.work_days_until
       (date.add_work_days n)` is `n` for any `n >= 0`, and
       `(date.add_work_days n).work_days_until date` is `-n` for any `n < 0`.
    add_work_days self (days : Integer = 1) (holidays : Vector Date = []) -> Date_Time =
        ensure_in_epoch self <|
            self.date.add_work_days days holidays . to_date_time self.time_of_day self.zone

    ## ---
       aliases: [subtract duration, subtract period]
       group: Operators
       icon: time
       ---
       Subtracts an amount of time from this date-time to get another date-time.
       Produces a warning if the resulting date-time is before the Enso epoch start.

       ## Arguments
       - `amount`: The amount of time to subtract: a `Duration`, a `Period` for a
         custom number of years, months and days, or a `Time_Period` or `Date_Period`
         for one unit.

       ## Examples
       ### Subtract 1 year, 9 months and 5 hours
       ```
       time = Date_Time.new 2020 zone=Time_Zone.utc
       earlier = time - (Period.new years=1 months=9) - (Duration.new hours=5)
       # → 2018-03-31 19:00:00Z[UTC]
       ```

       ### Subtract a quarter
       ```
       time = Date_Time.new 2020 zone=Time_Zone.utc
       earlier = time - Date_Period.Quarter
       # → 2019-10-01 00:00:00Z[UTC]
       ```
    @amount (make_date_time_period_selector include_custom=True)
    - self (amount : Duration | Period | Time_Period | Date_Period) -> Date_Time ! Time_Error =
        result = case amount of
            duration : Duration ->
                Panic.catch ArithmeticException (Date_Helpers.date_time_minus self duration) (err -> Error.throw (Time_Error.Error err.getMessage))
            period : Period ->
                Time_Utils.datetime_adjust self Time_Utils.AdjustOp.MINUS period.internal_period
            time_period : Time_Period ->
                self - time_period.to_duration
            date_period : Date_Period ->
                self - date_period.to_period
        ensure_in_epoch result result

    ## ---
       private: true
       ---
       Convert to a display representation of this Date_Time.
    to_display_text self -> Text =
        time_format = if self.nanosecond include_milliseconds=True == 0 then "HH:mm:ss" else "HH:mm:ss.fff"
        zone_format = if self.zone == Time_Zone.system then "" else "'['TT']'"
        self.format "yyyy-MM-dd "+time_format+zone_format

    ## ---
       private: true
       group: convert
       icon: enso_logo
       ---
       Convert the value to a corresponding Enso code representation.

       ## Examples
       ### Getting the Enso code of the date 29-October-2024 12:34.

       ```
             (Date_Time.new 2024 10 29 12 34).pretty
       ```
       Returns a Text

                Date_Time.new 2024 10 29 12 34
    pretty self -> Text =
        parts = Vector.build builder->
            builder.append ("Date_Time.new " + self.year.to_text + " " + self.month.to_text + " " + self.day.to_text)
            if self.hour != 0 then builder.append ((if builder.length!=1 then " hour=" else " ") + self.hour.to_text)
            if self.minute != 0 then builder.append ((if builder.length!=2 then " minute=" else " ") + self.minute.to_text)
            if self.second != 0 then builder.append ((if builder.length!=3 then " second=" else " ") + self.second.to_text)
            if self.millisecond != 0 then builder.append ((if builder.length!=4 then " millisecond=" else " ") + self.millisecond.to_text)
            if self.microsecond != 0 then builder.append ((if builder.length!=5 then " microsecond=" else " ") + self.microsecond.to_text)
            if self.nanosecond != 0 then builder.append ((if builder.length!=6 then " nanosecond=" else " ") + self.nanosecond.to_text)
            if self.zone != Time_Zone.system then builder.append ((if builder.length!=7 then " zone=(" else " (") + self.zone.pretty + ")")
        parts.join ""

    ## ---
       private: true
       ---
       Convert to a JavaScript Object representing a Date_Time.

       ## Examples
       ### Convert the current time to a JS_Object.

       ```
             example_to_json = Date_Time.now.to_js_object
       ```
    to_js_object self -> JS_Object =
        type_pair = ["type", "Date_Time"]
        cons_pair = ["constructor", "new"]
        JS_Object.from_pairs [type_pair, cons_pair, ["year", self.year], ["month", self.month], ["day", self.day], ["hour", self.hour], ["minute", self.minute], ["second", self.second], ["nanosecond", self.nanosecond include_milliseconds=True], ["zone", self.zone]]

    ## ---
       group: Conversions
       icon: convert
       ---
       Formats this date-time as text using the provided format specifier.

       ## Arguments
       - `format`: A pattern describing how to format the text, or a
         `Date_Time_Formatter`.

       ## Examples
       ### Format a date-time with the month's name
       ```
       time = Date_Time.new 2020 10 8 16 41 13 zone=Time_Zone.utc
       text = time.format "d MMMM yyyy h:mm a"
       # → "8 October 2020 4:41 PM"
       ```

       ### Format a date-time with a short weekday and month
       ```
       time = Date_Time.new 2020 10 8 16 41 13 zone=Time_Zone.utc
       text = time.format "ddd MMM d (HH:mm)"
       # → "Thu Oct 8 (16:41)"
       ```

       ### Format a date-time with its offset and zone
       ```
       time = Date_Time.parse "2020-10-08T16:41:13+03:00[Europe/Moscow]"
       text = time.format "yyyy-MM-dd'T'HH:mm:ssZZZZZ'['TT']'"
       # → "2020-10-08T16:41:13+03:00[Europe/Moscow]"
       ```

       ### Format a date-time in a particular locale
       ```
       time = Date_Time.new 2020 6 21 16 41 13 zone=Time_Zone.utc
       formatter = Date_Time_Formatter.from "d. MMMM yyyy" (Locale.new "fr")
       text = time.format formatter
       # → "21. juin 2020"
       ```

       ## Remarks

       ### Pattern Syntax
       If the pattern is provided as `Text`, it is parsed using the format
       described below. See `Date_Time_Formatter` for more options.
       - y: Year. The number of pattern letters determines the minimum number of
       digits.
       - y: The year using any number of digits.
       - yy: The year, using at most two digits. The default range is 1950-2049,
       but this can be changed by including the end year in braces e.g.
       `yy{2099}`.
       - yyyy: The year, using exactly four digits.
       - M: Month of year. The number of pattern letters determines the format:
       - M: Any number (1-12).
       - MM: Month number with zero padding required (01-12).
       - MMM: Short name of the month (Jan-Dec).
       - MMMM: Full name of the month (January-December).
       The month names depend on the selected locale.
       - d: Day. The number of pattern letters determines the format:
       - d: Any number (1-31).
       - dd: Day number with zero padding required (01-31).
       - ddd: Short name of the day of week (Mon-Sun).
       - dddd: Full name of the day of week (Monday-Sunday).
       The weekday names depend on the selected locale.
       Both day of week and day of month may be included in a single pattern - in
       such case the day of week is used as a sanity check.
       - Q: Quarter of year.
       If only year and quarter are provided in the pattern, when parsing a date,
       the result will be the first day of that quarter.
       - H: 24h hour of day (0-23).
       - h: 12h hour of day (0-12). The `a` pattern is needed to disambiguate
       between AM and PM.
       - m: Minute of hour.
       - s: Second of minute.
       - f: Fractional part of the second. The number of pattern letters
       determines the number of digits. If one letter is used, any number of
       digits will be accepted.
       - a: AM/PM marker.
       - T: If repeated 3 or less times - Time zone ID (e.g. Europe/Warsaw, Z,
       -08:30), otherwise - Time zone name (e.g. Central European Time, CET).
       - Z: Zone offset.
       - Z, ZZ, ZZZ: A short offset form (+HHmm).
       No offset is indicated by "+0000". This can be customized by setting an
       alternative no offset string in curly braces, e.g. `zz{Z}`.
       - ZZZZ: Localized offset (e.g. GMT-08:00).
       - ZZZZZ: A full offset form (+HH:mm:ss).
       No offset is indicated by "Z". This can be customized as above, e.g.
       `ZZZZZ{0}`.
    @format make_date_time_format_selector
    format self (format : Date_Time_Formatter) -> Text =
        format.format_date_time self

    ## ---
       aliases: [greater than]
       group: Operators
       icon: operators
       ---
       Checks if this date-time is after `that` date-time.

       ## Arguments
       - `that`: The date-time to compare with.

       ## Examples
       ### A date-time compared with another
       ```
       time = Date_Time.new 2020 12 23 11 45 zone=Time_Zone.utc
       other = Date_Time.new 2020 12 23 9 15 zone=Time_Zone.utc
       result = time > other
       # → True
       ```

       ## Remarks

       ### The `that` argument
       This argument has no date-time picker. Connect a date-time, or type one such as
       `Date_Time.new 2020 1 1`.
    > self that = Comparable.> self=self that

    ## ---
       aliases: [greater than or equal]
       group: Operators
       icon: operators
       ---
       Checks if this date-time is the same as, or after, `that` date-time.

       ## Arguments
       - `that`: The date-time to compare with.

       ## Examples
       ### A date-time compared with another
       ```
       time = Date_Time.new 2020 12 23 11 45 zone=Time_Zone.utc
       other = Date_Time.new 2020 12 23 11 45 zone=Time_Zone.utc
       result = time >= other
       # → True
       ```

       ## Remarks

       ### The `that` argument
       This argument has no date-time picker. Connect a date-time, or type one such as
       `Date_Time.new 2020 1 1`.
    >= self that = Comparable.>= self=self that

    ## ---
       aliases: [less than]
       group: Operators
       icon: operators
       ---
       Checks if this date-time is before `that` date-time.

       ## Arguments
       - `that`: The date-time to compare with.

       ## Examples
       ### A date-time compared with another
       ```
       time = Date_Time.new 2020 12 23 11 45 zone=Time_Zone.utc
       other = Date_Time.new 2020 12 23 9 15 zone=Time_Zone.utc
       result = time < other
       # → False
       ```

       ## Remarks

       ### The `that` argument
       This argument has no date-time picker. Connect a date-time, or type one such as
       `Date_Time.new 2020 1 1`.
    < self that = Comparable.< self=self that

    ## ---
       aliases: [less than or equal]
       group: Operators
       icon: operators
       ---
       Checks if this date-time is the same as, or before, `that` date-time.

       ## Arguments
       - `that`: The date-time to compare with.

       ## Examples
       ### A date-time compared with another
       ```
       time = Date_Time.new 2020 12 23 11 45 zone=Time_Zone.utc
       other = Date_Time.new 2020 12 23 11 45 zone=Time_Zone.utc
       result = time <= other
       # → True
       ```

       ## Remarks

       ### The `that` argument
       This argument has no date-time picker. Connect a date-time, or type one such as
       `Date_Time.new 2020 1 1`.
    <= self that = Comparable.<= self=self that

## ---
   private: true
   ---
   The choices for a period of a Date_Time: each `Date_Period`, then each
   `Time_Period`, written autoscoped (`..Day`, `..Hour`). The two types share no
   constructor name, so the engine's union check (which tries each member in
   turn) resolves each one: `..Day` is always `Date_Period.Day`. With
   `include_custom`, also `Period.new` and `Duration.new`, for an amount added
   to or taken from it.
make_date_time_period_selector (include_custom:Boolean=False) -> Widget =
    date_periods = ["Year", "Quarter", "Month", "Week", "Day"].map name->
        code = ".." + name
        Option code code
    time_periods = ["Hour", "Minute", "Second", "Millisecond", "Microsecond", "Nanosecond"].map name->
        code = ".." + name
        Option code code
    custom = if include_custom then [Option "Period.new" "(Period.new)", Option "Duration.new" "(Duration.new)"] else []
    Widget.Single_Choice values=(date_periods + time_periods + custom)

## ---
   private: true
   ---
Date_Time.from (that:JS_Object) =
    ## Must have year, month, day, hour and minute. Optional for second, nanosecond and zone.
    case that.get "type" == "Date_Time" && ["year", "month", "day", "hour", "minute"].all that.contains_key of
        True ->
            time_zone = if that.contains_key "zone" . not then Time_Zone.system else that.get "zone" . into Time_Zone
            if that.contains_key "nanosecond" && (that.contains_key "second" . not) then Error.throw (Illegal_Argument.Error "Invalid JS_Object for Date_Time.from.") else
              Date_Time.new (that.get "year") (that.get "month") (that.get "day") (that.get "hour") (that.get "minute") (that.get "second" 0) nanosecond=(that.get "nanosecond" 0) zone=time_zone
        False -> Error.throw (Illegal_Argument.Error "Invalid JS_Object for Date_Time.from.")
